  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 371fcced5420eda46b9cb39fda4431ce, type: 3}
  m_Name: Cornflower
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::FlowerData
  id: cornflower
  displayName: Cornflower
  scientificName: Centaurea cyanus
  markerImageName: cornflower
  description: Cornflower is a slender annual wildflower famous for its vivid blue,
    fringed flower heads. It usually grows between 30 and 90 cm tall and produces
    narrow, grey-green leaves. Although blue is its traditional colour, cultivated
    varieties may also produce white, pink, purple or dark-red flowers. It attracts
    pollinators and is often grown in wildflower gardens and for cut-flower arrangements.
  origin: Central and eastern Mediterranean region
  meaning: Hope, devotion, delicacy, remembrance and faithful love. The blue cornflower
    is also associated with resilience and positive anticipation.
  habitat: Open fields, meadows, roadsides, grasslands and traditionally cultivated
    cereal fields with well-drained soil.
  birthflower: July, in some modern birth-flower traditions
  floweringSeason: Late spring to mid-summer; may continue into early autumn if faded
    flowers are removed
  sunlight: Full sun. It flowers best with at least six hours of direct sunlight
    each day.
  watering: Water regularly while seeds and young plants establish. Mature plants
    prefer moderate watering and can tolerate short dry periods. Allow the upper
    soil layer to dry slightly between waterings.
  difficulty: Easy
  flowerImage: {fileID: 0}
